<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
  Big and Small's humanlike-ThingDef generator (HumanlikeAnimalGenerator.GenerateAndRegisterHumanlikeAnimal)
  filters every comp on the original animal/mechanoid ThingDef against the merged
  compWhitelist/tabWhitelist/modExtensionWhitelist of every loaded HumanlikeAnimalSettings
  def - anything not listed is silently dropped when it builds the sapient version's
  ThingDef. It ships its own DefaultHumanlikeAnimalSettings (see Big and Small's own
  Defs/Presets/PawnExtensionPresets/HumanlikeAnimalGenerator.xml) covering vanilla/VEF
  comps, but doesn't know about mech-queen-style "carrier" comps, so a sapient Work
  Queen ([AV] Framework) or War Queen (vanilla Biotech) loses its steel reserve and
  urchin-release gizmo entirely - the comps backing them just never make it onto the
  generated ThingDef in the first place. All HumanlikeAnimalSettings defs get merged
  together (see HumanlikeAnimalSettings.AllHASettings), so this only needs to add the
  missing entries, not touch Big and Small's own def.

  Vanilla's War Queen (Mech_Warqueen, Races_Mechanoids_SuperHeavy.xml) combines both
  the steel reserve and the urchin spawning into one CompProperties_MechCarrier.
  [AV] Framework's Work Queen splits the same idea across two comps instead:
  CompProperties_MechReloadableResourceHolder (the reserve) and
  CompProperties_MechCarrierChoice (the release gizmo, letting the player pick which
  urchin type to spawn rather than always the same one).

  Whitelisting the comps only gets them back onto the pawn - it doesn't make their
  gizmos visible again on its own. RimWorld.CompMechCarrier and
  AV_Framework.CompMechCarrierChoice/CompMechReloadableResourceHolder both gate their
  CompGetGizmosExtra on TWO separate conditions, confirmed by decompile:
  pawn.IsColonyMech (fixed generically by Pawn_IsColonyMech_Patch.cs, since Big and Small
  deliberately clears it for every sapient mechanoid) AND pawn.GetOverseer() == null
  (fixed per-comp by OverseerGizmoSuppressionPatches.cs, which has to be told about each
  comp by name). Missing the second half for RimWorld.CompMechCarrier specifically once
  caused a real bug - the War Queen's IsColonyMech read correctly, but its steel
  reserve/urchin buttons stayed hidden anyway, since nothing was suppressing GetOverseer
  for that particular comp. Adding a new mechanoid comp to the whitelist above that has
  this same two-condition shape needs both fixes, not just the IsColonyMech one.
  -->
  <BigAndSmall.HumanlikeAnimalSettings>
    <defName>SapientMechanoidFix_MechQueenWhitelist</defName>
    <compWhitelist>
      <!-- Vanilla Biotech - War Queen -->
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_MechCarrier</li>

      <!-- [AV] Framework - Work Queen -->
      <li>AV_Framework.CompProperties_MechReloadableResourceHolder</li>
      <li>AV_Framework.CompProperties_MechCarrierChoice</li>

      <!-- Vanilla Biotech - auto-repair toggle (all mechanoids). Unlike the two
           above, this one is just a bool field with a gizmo - no lifecycle-hook fix
           needed for the comp itself. See WorkGiver_RepairMech_HasJobOnThing_Patch.cs
           and JobDriver_RepairMech_MakeNewToils_Patch.cs for what it actually takes
           to make auto-repair functional for a sapient mechanoid, since vanilla's own
           repair WorkGiver/JobDriver both assume every mechanoid has a power (energy)
           need, which sapient mechanoids deliberately don't. -->
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_MechRepairable</li>

      <!-- Vanilla Biotech - War Queen's mounted turret gun (Gun_ChargeBlasterTurret).
           Without this the sapient clone had no ranged attack at all - just its innate
           melee "head" tool. CompTurretGun.PostPostMake() is what actually constructs
           the gun Thing, and RaceMorpher_AddMissingComps_Patch.cs already calls
           PostPostMake/PostSpawnSetup on every newly-added comp, so this "just works"
           without any comp-specific patch. Its only two mechanoid-specific gates
           (fire-at-will check, toggle-turret gizmo visibility) both key off
           Pawn.IsColonyMechPlayerControlled, already fixed by
           Pawn_IsColonyMechPlayerControlled_Patch.cs. -->
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_TurretGun</li>

      <!-- Reactive armor (steel-scaled damage reduction) - see
           CompSapientMechReactiveArmor.cs and Patches/ReactiveArmor.xml, which is what
           actually adds this comp to Mech_Warqueen/AV_Mech_Workerqueen in the first
           place, since it isn't a comp either ThingDef ships with natively. -->
      <li>SapientMechanoidFix.CompProperties_SapientMechReactiveArmor</li>

      <!-- Reactive armor (charge-limited hit-triggered immunity) - see
           CompSapientMechHitArmor.cs. Independent of the comp above; both run
           together. -->
      <li>SapientMechanoidFix.CompProperties_SapientMechHitArmor</li>

      <!-- Lady's custom head texture, restored by grafting Fortified's own head render
           node onto whatever tree Big and Small actually assigns her (BS_HumanlikeAnimal
           - a tree with no head node at all) rather than by forcing her original
           "HumanlikeMech" tree, which crashed rendering for every pawn on the map (see
           CompSapientHumanlikeMechHead.cs and Patches/HumanlikeMechHead.xml, which is
           what actually adds this comp to DMS_Mech_Lady in the first place). -->
      <li>SapientMechanoidFix.CompProperties_SapientHumanlikeMechHead</li>

      <!-- Glitterworld Destroyer 5 - War Queen's "kill all urchins" button. Its
           gizmo only checks Owner.Faction == Faction.OfPlayer, no mechanoid-specific
           gate at all, so this needs nothing beyond the whitelist entry itself. Only
           ever present if GD5 is installed and has patched Mech_Warqueen - harmless
           whitelist entry otherwise, since it just never matches anything. -->
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_AllDead</li>

      <!-- Glitterworld Destroyer 5 - War Queen's auto-release-urchins toggle
           (periodically calls CompMechCarrier.TrySpawnPawns() on its own). Same
           direct MechanitorUtility.GetOverseer(pawn) == null gizmo-visibility gate as
           AV Framework's two comps - see OverseerGizmoSuppressionPatches.cs, which
           reuses the exact same suppression-window fix for this comp's
           CompGetGizmosExtra too. -->
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_AttackMode</li>

      <!-- Vanilla Biotech/Odyssey - shared library comps used by several unrelated
           mods' worker/scout mechanoids (Dead Man's Switch's drones, [AV] Framework's
           Work Queen urchins, Alpha Mechs, Mechanoid Invaders, Mechanoids: Total
           Warfare), not just Dead Man's Switch - whitelisting the vanilla class once
           covers all of them, since none of them define their own copy. Both
           confirmed clean by full source read: no IsColonyMech/GetOverseer/needs.energy
           checks anywhere. -->
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_MechPowerCell</li>
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_HunterDrone</li>

      <!-- Dead Man's Switch (Aoba.DeadManSwitch.Core/.AncientCorps/.Expedition) - most
           of its mechanoid-specific comps actually live in a shared dependency,
           "Fortified Feature Framework" (AOBA.Framework), not in DMS's own DLL. All
           confirmed clean by full source read (gizmo/action gates use Faction checks,
           IsColonyMechPlayerControlled, or IsPlayerControlled - never GetOverseer or
           RaceProps.IsMechanoid directly) except CompProperties_DeadManSwitch's own
           JobDriver, which needed a separate fix - see
           Fortified_JobDriver_RepairSelf_MakeNewToils_Patch.cs. -->
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_VehicleWeapon</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompPropertiesMultipleTurretGun</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_MeleeSweep</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_Camouflage</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_MechApparel</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_WeaponImprint</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_MechPlatform</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_Drone</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompExplosiveOnMelee</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_Floating</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompPropertiesInteracte</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_DeadManSwitch</li>
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_CommandRelay</li>

      <!-- Vanilla Expanded Framework - a different "Floating" comp than Fortified's
           own (same name, unrelated classes), used by Dead Man's Switch - Expedition's
           Automatroid. Also confirmed clean. -->
      <li>VEF.AnimalBehaviours.CompProperties_Floating</li>

      <!-- Dead Man's Switch - a working-state cosmetic fleck effect (steam/sparks), found
           in a fuller re-scan of the whole roster. Its IsActive check reads
           MechanitorUtility.GetMechWorkMode(pawn), which is null-safe all the way down and
           simply returns null for a sapient mech (no Overseer relation) rather than
           throwing - null compares as "not SelfShutdown/Recharge", so the effect just
           always shows instead of only while genuinely working. Cosmetic-only, no crash
           risk, whitelisted as-is. -->
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_FlyingFleckThrower</li>

      <!-- Fortified Feature Framework - not currently used by any Dead Man's Switch
           mechanoid, whitelisted preemptively alongside their code fixes (see
           Fortified_CompMechDeactivate_CompGetGizmosExtra_Patch.cs and
           Fortified_CompMechanitorRange_CompTickRare_Patch.cs) in case another
           Fortified-consuming mod attaches either later. -->
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_MechDeactivate</li>
      <li>Fortified.HediffCompProperties_MechanitorRange</li>

      <!-- Mechanoids: Total Warfare (Nyar.NCLvsTW) - 23 of its 26 mechanoids carry a
           non-trivial comp, spread across four assemblies (NCLvsTW.dll, its bundled
           NyearsModPackOne.dll, NCLWorm.dll, TowerLaserDefense.dll). Every one of them
           was checked against full decompiled source for the three recurring bug
           patterns (direct MechanitorUtility.GetOverseer(pawn)/pawn.IsColonyMech(PlayerControlled)
           checks, and pawn.needs.energy null derefs) - none were found. All gizmo-bearing
           comps here gate purely on `parent.Faction == Faction.OfPlayer`, so unlike Dead
           Man's Switch/Fortified this mod needed zero Harmony patches, just whitelisting.
           One exception, NOT whitelisted here pending a design call - see
           NCL.CompProperties_TameableMech below. -->
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_MechAutoRecovery</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_MechanoidShield</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_WeaponGiver</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_TurretGunPawnOnly</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_TurretGun_GroundTarget</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_AllDead</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_AlwaysWork</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_SilverSummon</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_ClearOwnerProjectiles</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_OnDeathEvent</li>

      <!-- TW_Mech_Birgus's smokepop-on-hit toggle - initially left off the list above.
           An earlier pass classified NCL.CompProperties_SmokepopOnHit as already
           covered by Big and Small's own default whitelist and excluded it from that
           scan on that basis; that assumption was wrong (confirmed by testing - the
           toggle is lost on the sapient version), so it's whitelisted here instead. No
           code fix needed - the comp itself gates purely on Faction.OfPlayer, same as
           the rest of this mod's comps. -->
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_SmokepopOnHit</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_AntiInvisibilityField</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_FearAura</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_FearFire</li>
      <li>NCL.CompProperties_IgnoreCommandRange</li>

      <!-- RimWorld.CompProperties_AutoMechSpawner: despite the unqualified-looking name
           this is one of NCLvsTW.dll's own classes, just declared inside a literal
           `namespace RimWorld { }` block in its source (confirmed by decompile) rather
           than a vanilla type - full name really is RimWorld.CompProperties_AutoMechSpawner. -->
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_AutoMechSpawner</li>

      <!-- NyearsModPackOne.dll, bundled inside the Total Warfare mod folder (own
           dependency, not a separate Workshop item) - powers the Dinergate/Scutiger
           drone-release mechanoids. CompGetGizmosExtra gates only on Faction.OfPlayer;
           PostExposeData already Scribe_Deep's its innerContainer correctly (unlike AV
           Framework's comp), so no innerContainer-after-load backstop is needed either. -->
      <li>NyarsModPackOne.CompProperties_DroneCarrier</li>

      <!-- NCLWorm.dll - NCL_MechWorm's ability/shield kit (used by the Archowrom Titan
           and the Shell Fortification mech family). All confirmed clean by full source
           read. -->
      <li>NCLWorm.CompProperties_BattleCrush</li>
      <li>NCLWorm.CompProperties_CauseHediff_AoEAndRing</li>
      <li>NCLWorm.CompProperties_DamageAbsorbedAndBoom</li>
      <li>NCLWorm.CompProperties_ShieldNCLWorm</li>
      <li>NCLWorm.CompProperties_NCLWormSkillList</li>

      <!-- TowerLaserDefense.dll - shared turret/laser-defense ability system used by
           several Total Warfare mechanoids (PillBug, MechTeslaCentipede, both Militor
           psycasts variants, Shell Fortification family). Gizmo gates only on
           Faction.OfPlayer. -->
      <li>TowerLaserDefense.CompProperties_LaserDefence</li>

      <!-- Glitterworld Destroyer 5 (fxz.glitterworlddestroyer.mk5) - 11 of its 15
           mechanoids carry a non-trivial comp; this now covers all 11 except
           Mech_Annihilator (Odyssey) - see README's Planned features for why that one's
           deferred. GD3.CompProperties_AllDead and GD3.CompProperties_AttackMode (War
           Queen's add-on buttons) were already whitelisted above.
           Mech_Observer/Mech_CataphractCentipede's weapon-swap button both needed the
           same GetOverseer(pawn) == null gizmo/click-time fix as AV Framework/this
           mod's other GD5 entry - see OverseerGizmoSuppressionPatches.cs.
           Mech_CataphractCentipede's other comp, CompDrawBattery, dereferences
           pawn.needs.energy with no null check and crashes every frame while a sapient
           mech is drafted - see GD3_CompDrawBattery_PostDraw_Patch.cs. -->
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_ObserverLink</li>
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_ChangeWeaponB</li>
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_FloatingCannon</li>
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_Invisibility</li>
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_HitArmor</li>
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_BlackApocriton</li>
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_ArchoDrone</li>
      <li>GD3.CompDrawBattery</li>
      <li>GD3.CompBlackFaction</li>
      <li>GD3.CompTeleport_Attack</li>
      <li>GD3.CompTeleport_Defend</li>

      <!-- Glitterworld Destroyer 5 also patches comps onto several VANILLA mechanoid
           ThingDefs (not just its own Mech_* roster) - see
           Biotech/Patches/Patch_BiotechMechanoids.xml. Found while chasing a user report
           that the vanilla Apocriton's abilities were broken on a sapient conversion.
           All three confirmed clean by full source read (no GetOverseer/needs.energy/
           custom-subclass checks). CompMilitor only activates on a specific
           quest-scripted Militor instance and is otherwise inert. -->
      <li>GD3.CompMilitor</li>
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_Apocriton</li>
      <li>GD3.CompProperties_PsychicAttack</li>

      <!-- Vanilla/shared comps used by several of GD5's "black" mechanoids
           (Mech_BlackLancer's shield belt, Mech_BlackLegionary's bullet shield,
           Mech_BlackApocriton's attached effecter) - whitelisted explicitly in case
           Big and Small's own defaults don't already cover them, same defensive
           approach as RimWorld.CompProperties_MechPowerCell above.

           BUG FIXED: these three were missing their "RimWorld." namespace prefix, so
           they never actually matched anything - HumanlikeAnimalGenerator's whitelist
           check is an exact, case-insensitive string match against the comp's full
           namespace-qualified type name (confirmed by decompiled source:
           `compWhitelist.Contains(x.GetType().ToString(), StringComparer.OrdinalIgnoreCase)`),
           not a suffix/contains match - "CompProperties_Shield" alone never matches
           "RimWorld.CompProperties_Shield". This silently stripped every sapient
           mechanoid's personal energy shield across every mod that grants one this way
           (confirmed on GD5's Black Lancer/Black Legionary; likely affects any other
           mod's mechs using the same vanilla comp for their shields, not just GD5's). -->
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_Shield</li>
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_ProjectileInterceptor</li>
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_Effecter</li>

      <!-- Reinforced Mechanoid 2 (Mlie.ReinforcedMechanoid2) - the original pass here only
           scanned ReinforcedMechanoids.dll's own comp classes and covered RM_Mech_Caretaker,
           RM_Mech_Gremlin, RM_Mech_Harpy, RM_Mech_Zealot, RM_Mech_Wraith, RM_Mech_Locust
           (and their VFE_-prefixed duplicates). A later, fuller pass (triggered by chasing
           the shield bug below) found RM2 actually has several more mechanoids with comps
           that scan never reached: RM_Mech_Behemoth, RM_Mech_Falcon, RM_Mech_Marshal,
           RM_Mech_Sentinel, plus modExtensions used across most of the roster. RM_Mech_
           Vulture, RM_Mech_Ranger, and RM_Mech_Matriarch carry no comps at all - nothing
           to whitelist there. RM_Droid_Buffer and RM_Droid_Spartan's
           ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_Machine is still NOT covered - it ties into
           a charging-station/Need_Power framework that needs more investigation before
           it's safe to whitelist (a sapient droid retaining that need with no way to
           refill it could be an unintended-death risk) - see README's Planned features.
           Every entry below is confirmed clean by full source read - every comp here
           resolves the parent as a plain Pawn and gates purely on faction/health/distance/
           comp-presence, no GetOverseer/needs.energy/custom-subclass checks anywhere. -->
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_ExplodeIfNoOtherFactionPawns</li>
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_Explosions</li>
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_GrenadeThrower</li>
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_PawnJumper</li>
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_Invisibility</li>

      <!-- RM_Mech_Behemoth/RM_Mech_Wraith's lifestage-graphic-swap comp (changes
           appearance based on missing parts/health thresholds - reads pawn.ageTracker
           the same way any ordinary pawn's rendering does, nothing animal-specific). -->
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_ChangeGraphic</li>

      <!-- RM_Mech_Sentinel's berserk-on-low-health comp. -->
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_MentalStateOnDamage</li>

      <!-- RM_Mech_Wraith's idle ambient sound loop. -->
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_AmbientSound</li>

      <!-- Vanilla - RM_Mech_Caretaker's plain death-explosion comp, separate from
           ReinforcedMechanoids.CompProperties_Explosions above (Caretaker carries both).
           Not in Big and Small's own default whitelist either - checked directly. -->
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_Explosive</li>

      <!-- RM_Mech_Caretaker's actual "bubble shield" comp - missed in the pass above
           since it comes from Vanilla Expanded Framework (VEF.Apparels), not
           ReinforcedMechanoids.dll itself, so it wasn't in the same source file as the
           rest of Caretaker's comps. This alone explains the "shield doesn't affect
           sapient mechs" report - it was being silently stripped by the whitelist
           filter, so a sapient Caretaker had no shield at all, not a shield with a
           targeting bug. Confirmed clean by full source read: the shield's damage
           interception is purely positional (checks which cells/things are within its
           radius), no race/faction/mechanoid-type filtering anywhere.

           RM_Mech_Marshal has its own, separate personal shield - VEF.Apparels.
           CompProperties_ShieldBubble, a different class than Caretaker's ShieldField
           (same name, unrelated to vanilla's own CompProperties_Shield fixed elsewhere in
           this file). Found in the same pass as the shield bug above. Also confirmed
           clean by full source read. -->
      <li>VEF.Apparels.CompProperties_ShieldField</li>
      <li>VEF.Apparels.CompProperties_ShieldBubble</li>

      <!-- Alpha Mechs (sarg.alphamechs) - 9 of its 40 mechanoids carry a non-trivial
           comp, covering AM_Aura/Daggersnout/Demolisher/Fireworm/Goliath/Phalanx/
           Siegebreaker (CompProperties_ChangeDef) and AM_Infernus
           (CompProperties_TargetingBeamBlue) - AM_Mech_Guttersnipe's
           CompProperties_MechPowerCell was already covered generically above.
           CompProperties_ChangeDef only ever fires for non-player-faction pawns
           (defection/reskin flavor for wild mechs), so it's inert for a sapient mech
           either way; CompProperties_TargetingBeamBlue is just a cosmetic aiming-laser
           overlay. Both confirmed clean by full source read. Also whitelists the shared
           VEF regeneration comp Aura uses, confirmed clean the same way. -->
      <li>AlphaMechs.CompProperties_ChangeDef</li>
      <li>AlphaMechs.CompProperties_TargetingBeamBlue</li>
      <li>VEF.AnimalBehaviours.CompProperties_Regeneration</li>

      <!-- Alpha Mechs also ships a second, much larger mechanoid roster under its own
           "Mods/Biotech" subfolder (Legate, Optio, Apoptosis, Lux, Bellicor, Artilleron,
           Blitzkrieg, Guttersnipe, Infernus, MasterChef, Munifex, Polychoron,
           PristineAssembler, PristineSlurrypede, PristineStrider, Sagittarius,
           Siegemelter, Starfire, TurboCleaner, WarEmpress) that the original comp scan
           missed entirely - found while chasing a user report that shields don't survive
           sapience. Legate and Optio's ProjectileInterceptor and Apoptosis's Effecter are
           covered by the RimWorld.CompProperties_ProjectileInterceptor/CompProperties_Effecter
           fix above; WarEmpress's CompProperties_MechCarrier and Apoptosis/Infernus/
           WarEmpress's CompProperties_TurretGun were already covered generically. The two
           entries below are the Guttersnipe's dormancy comps. CompWakeUpDormant only
           wakes non-player-faction pawns, never touches a player's own sapient one, but
           CompCanBeDormant needed its own follow-up fix - see
           CompCanBeDormant_Awake_Patch.cs, which is what actually stops a sapient mech
           from showing the vanilla sleeping "Z" icon forever (its wokeUpTick, the field
           that normally gets set once during a real mechanoid's construction, is never
           touched when the comp is only added back by this whitelist entry rather than
           genuinely spawned with it). The rest of this second roster hasn't been checked
           yet - see README's Planned features. -->
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_CanBeDormant</li>
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_WakeUpDormant</li>

      <!-- [AV] Mechtech (Veltaris.Mechtech) - the Fluoid's neurofoam/neutroamine/hemogen
           paste production comp. requiresPower=false and its two scaling stats
           (WorkSpeedGlobal, BloodFiltration) are ordinary StatDefs a sapient pawn already
           has, so it's confirmed clean by source read - no mechanoid/overseer/energy
           gating anywhere in AV_Framework.Comp_SelectableSpawner itself. The Scrapper and
           Tarantula's own [AV] Mechtech comps/abilities needed no whitelisting (either
           already covered by the Work Queen's CompProperties_MechReloadableResourceHolder
           entry above, or are AbilityDef comps, which aren't filtered by this whitelist at
           all - only ThingDef comps are). The Reshaper isn't supported - see the mod's
           README Known Issues. -->
      <li>AV_Framework.CompProperties_SelectableSpawner</li>

      <!-- [AV] Mechtech - the Companoid sphere's "guiding protocol" buff-aura comp and its
           own radio/mote-effect comp. Confirmed clean by actual shipped source (both mods
           ship .cs directly, no decompiling needed) - Comp_GiveHediffsWhenAlive's only
           mechanoid-control check is commented out in the source itself, and
           AV_Mechtech.Comp_RadioMech has no mechanoid/overseer/energy gating anywhere. -->
      <li>AV_Framework.CompProperties_GiveHediffsWhenAlive</li>
      <li>AV_Mechtech.CompProperties_RadioMech</li>

      <!-- Ascension Megacorp (AOBA.USAC.Core) - shared by every USAC mech (Cobalt, Gonk,
           Omaha, Paraman, Rocky): CompProperties_MechReadiness backs their "readiness"
           need (Need_Readiness) in place of vanilla's energy bar, refilled with industrial
           components. Comp and need are both confirmed clean by full source read - no
           mechanoid/overseer gating in either, Need_Readiness only checks that its comp is
           present. The comp being stripped by the whitelist filter is what was making the
           need (and the resupply work) disappear entirely; the resupply WorkGiver's own
           separate IsMechanoid gate needed its own fix too - see
           USAC_WorkGiver_ResupplyMech_HasJobOnThing_Patch.cs. CompProperties_BulletDeflect
           (Omaha's melee deflection) and CompProperties_MechWreck (leaves a wreck prop on
           death) are also confirmed clean, no gating at all. Fortified.CompProperties_
           MechDeactivate/CompProperties_VehicleWeapon/CompProperties_MechApparel are
           already whitelisted above from the Dead Man's Switch work and cover Cobalt/Gonk/
           Omaha/Paraman's weapons and Rocky's apparel-mech gear the same way. -->
      <li>USAC.CompProperties_MechReadiness</li>
      <li>USAC.CompProperties_BulletDeflect</li>
      <li>USAC.CompProperties_MechWreck</li>

      <!-- Ascension Megacorp - the shared mech base's paint/camo comp. Confirmed clean by
           full source read (Fortified.CompProperties_Paintable is a colonist-facing color
           picker gizmo, no mechanoid-specific logic anywhere). -->
      <li>Fortified.CompProperties_Paintable</li>

      <!-- Mechanoid Upgrades (GoGaTio.MechanoidUpgrades) - its core comp is patched onto
           EVERY mechanoid ThingDef in the game (vanilla and modded alike) that has
           RimWorld.CompProperties_OverseerSubject in its own comps - see the mod's own
           Patches_UpgradeComp.xml, an xpath match against `comps/li[@Class =
           "CompProperties_OverseerSubject"]`, not a hardcoded mod list. That's every
           mechanitor-controllable mechanoid from every mod already covered in this file
           (all of them ultimately derive from vanilla's BaseMechanoid, which carries that
           comp). CompProperties_UpgradableMechanoid itself is confirmed clean by full
           source read - its gizmo/reload logic already keys off Pawn.IsColonyMechPlayerControlled,
           which Pawn_IsColonyMechPlayerControlled_Patch.cs already fixes generically.

           The actual blocker was the mod's own entry point: the float-menu option to walk
           a mech into its "Mech Upgrader" building checks pawn.RaceProps.IsMechanoid
           directly (false for a sapient mech, so the option to configure upgrades never
           even appeared) - see
           MU_FloatMenuOptionProvider_EnterUpgrader_AppliesInt_Patch.cs. -->
      <li>MU.CompProperties_UpgradableMechanoid</li>

      <!-- Needed for the above - CompUpgradableMechanoid's building-entry checks
           (Building_MechUpgrader.CanAcceptPawn/TickInterval) dereference
           pawn.OverseerSubject.State directly, which is null (crash) unless this vanilla
           comp is also whitelisted. This is also the exact comp behind the vanilla
           "mechanoid goes feral without an overseer" mechanic, which would otherwise fire
           for every sapient mech (they're deliberately never mechanitor-overseen) - see
           CompOverseerSubject_State_Patch.cs, which forces State to Overseen for a sapient
           mech specifically. That one change both prevents the feral risk and satisfies
           the Mech Upgrader's own "is this thing supervised" check the same way a real,
           actively-overseen mechanoid's would. -->
      <li>RimWorld.CompProperties_OverseerSubject</li>
    </compWhitelist>
    <tabWhitelist>
      <li>AV_Framework.ITab_MechQueen</li>

      <!-- Mechanoid Upgrades' own inspector tab for viewing/configuring installed
           upgrades - inserted into inspectorTabs at load time by
           CompProperties_UpgradableMechanoid.PostLoadSpecial, so it needs whitelisting
           here the same as any other tab. Purely comp-presence-gated
           (IsVisible checks HasComp<CompUpgradableMechanoid>), no mechanoid-type checks. -->
      <li>MU.ITab_MechUpgrades</li>
    </tabWhitelist>

    <!-- DMS_Mech_Lady is the one Dead Man's Switch mechanoid built on Fortified's own
         "humanlike mech" rendering system (thingClass Fortified.HumanlikeMech), rather
         than vanilla's mechanoid rendering - it needs its custom head texture
         (HumanlikeMechExtension.headGraphic/headGraphicHaired) to draw a face at all.
         HumanlikeAnimalGenerator filters modExtensions through this whitelist exactly
         like comps/tabs, and Lady's def was never added - so on the sapient version
         Extension resolves to null and PawnRenderNode_Head.GraphicFor/
         PawnRenderNodeWorker_Head.OffsetFor (both unconditional Extension.xxx
         dereferences) silently fail, and the head just never renders. MechWeaponExtension/
         TurretMannableExtension are whitelisted alongside it since they live on the same
         def and would otherwise be silently dropped the same way (weapon tech-level
         filtering, turret-manning eligibility) even though no symptom from those has been
         reported yet. -->
    <modExtensionWhitelist>
      <li>Fortified.HumanlikeMechExtension</li>
      <li>Fortified.MechWeaponExtension</li>
      <li>Fortified.TurretMannableExtension</li>

      <!-- [AV] Mechtech's Scrapper carries this marker extension (empty, no fields) to
           mark it eligible for mortar use - same silent-drop risk as the others above,
           whitelisted for the same reason even though no specific symptom was reported. -->
      <li>AV_Framework.MechCanUseMortar</li>

      <!-- Reinforced Mechanoid 2 - data-only extensions carried by most of the roster,
           found in the same fuller pass that caught the missing RM2 comps above.
           MechanoidExtension only affects mech-cluster spawn eligibility and hacked-mech
           power-need behavior (pawn-generation-time, not relevant once already sapient);
           AdditionalButcherProducts only affects corpse butchering yield; both are inert
           for a live sapient mech either way, whitelisted for consistency.
           EquipmentDrawPositionOffsetExtension (RM_Mech_Wraith) controls where its weapon
           is drawn relative to its body - cosmetic but worth keeping intact. -->
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.MechanoidExtension</li>
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.AdditionalButcherProducts</li>
      <li>ReinforcedMechanoids.EquipmentDrawPositionOffsetExtension</li>
    </modExtensionWhitelist>

    <!-- Do NOT also whitelist Lady's original "HumanlikeMech" render tree (tried once,
         reverted - see git history/changelog). That tree's body node is
         PawnRenderNode_AnimalPart, which calls Pawn.ageTracker.CurKindLifeStage - valid
         for the animal-style pawn Fortified originally built it for, but Big and Small's
         sapience conversion also flips RaceProps.Humanlike true, and CurKindLifeStage
         throws/returns null for a genuinely humanlike pawn. Forcing that tree crashed
         PawnRenderTree.TrySetupGraphIfNeeded for Lady specifically, which took every
         other pawn's rendering down with it (one uncaught exception mid-frame in
         DrawDynamicThings' shared loop). Letting Big and Small substitute its own
         humanlike-safe tree is the safe default; the modExtensionWhitelist entry above
         is unaffected by this revert and stays in place. Lady's head texture is still
         unsolved as of this revert - restoring it needs a fix that doesn't touch which
         render tree she uses at all. -->
  </BigAndSmall.HumanlikeAnimalSettings>

</Defs>
